Exercise 3.21 concerns the step neighbourhood \([Y,Z]=\{f\mid Y\,f\,Z\}\) used in the proof of Theorem 3.12, where curry is introduced by the biconditional \(X\ \mathrm{curry}(g)\ [Y,Z]\) if and only if \(X\cup Y\ g\ Z\). The exercise asks to prove that \([Y,Z]\) determines both endpoints when \(Z\neq\Delta_2\), by naming the least map in the step; to show that when \(Z=\Delta_2\) that same curry biconditional remains valid even though \(Y\) is not unique, because \(\Delta_1\ g\ \Delta_2\) always holds; and to give a criterion for identity of two step neighbourhoods of \(\mathcal{D}_1\to\mathcal{D}_2\). `leastStep` is the least approximable map of the one-element list \([(Y,Z)]\). It is a neighbourhood because `stepCons` checks the output intersection `interYs`: that intersection equals \(Z\) when the test input is contained in \(Y\) (`interYs_single_subset_eq`) and equals the codomain master otherwise (`interYs_single_not_subset_eq`). `leastStep_mem` places this least map in the step, and `leastStep_le` places it below every member. `leastStep_rel` says it relates \(Y'\) to \(Z'\) exactly when the single-step output intersection at \(Y'\) is contained in \(Z'\). `step_eq_of_ne_master` is the uniqueness claim: equal steps have equal least maps by `leastStep_eq_of_step_eq`, each least map therefore relates the other pair, the second output cannot be the master or else `step_master_right` and `step_eq_univ_iff` would force the first output to be the master, and if one input failed to sit inside the other the corresponding output intersection would be the master, so each coordinate yields two inclusions and hence an equality. `step_master_right` shows that if the output is the master then the step is the whole function space: membership in the step is the relation of the input to the master, every approximable map relates the master to the master, and monotonicity extends that relation along the inclusion of the input in the master, so \(Y\) is not recovered. `step_eq_iff` is the identity criterion, and with `step_eq_of_ne_master` and `step_master_right` it is `example_321`: \([Y,Z]=[Y',Z']\) if and only if both outputs equal \(\Delta_2\), or \(Y=Y'\) and \(Z=Z'\). The curry biconditional \(X\ \mathrm{curry}(g)\ [Y,\Delta_2]\leftrightarrow X\cup Y\ g\ \Delta_2\) is not stated or proved. **Theorem:** partial — determination of the endpoints when \(Z\neq\Delta_2\), failure of that determination when \(Z=\Delta_2\), and the identity criterion are proved, and the curry biconditional at \(Z=\Delta_2\) is absent. **Proof:** partial — uniqueness and the criterion are the least-step comparison above, and there is no argument that the curry clause survives when the step is the whole function space.
